% ---------- Abstract ----------
\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
Health insurers lose part of their spending to fraud, waste and abuse, and in the data studied the confirmed problems are discovered only after payment, through manual review and recovery. This report follows the Challenge-Based Learning (CBL) cycle. In the \emph{Engage} phase it frames the big idea, the essential question and the challenge. In the \emph{Investigate} phase it answers guiding questions grouped in five categories (ethical, legal, technical, data, and cause and consequence) through benchmarking, a literature review carried out jointly with Zayneb Troudi, method papers, the insurer's data and tutor supervision, and closes with a synthesis: the solution objective and a roadmap. In the \emph{Act} phase it documents the individual implementation: a leakage-free data-processing pipeline that turns 1{,}017{,}993 raw claim rows into an ML-ready dataset of 839{,}209 claims with 614 recoveries (0.073\%), and one solution built in depth, an \emph{adversarial self-play red-teaming} framework. In it a PPO fraudster agent, acting on CTGAN synthetic claims, attacks a static XGBoost defender built for self-play, deliberately without peer-group amount features; the evasive claims it discovers are used to retrain the defender, which reduces the attack success rate of the same frozen attacker from 90.3\% to 22.7\%. The report states the limits of this minimum viable product, in particular the weakness of the first defender, the narrow gain of retraining and the weak-label nature of ``not recovered'' claims.

