\begin{caution}[Which model is attacked?]
The attacker faces \texttt{defender\_v1}, a compact XGBoost model built for self-play: 81 encoded features and, on purpose, no peer-group amount features (holdout PR-AUC 0.0339). The attack success rates describe this deliberately reduced defender, not a production detector.
\end{caution}

\noindent\textbf{Main finding.} The detector's weakness, rather than the attacker's sophistication, was most likely the primary problem: a random policy already succeeded 86\% of the time. One retraining round with 337 discovered claims cut the same attacker's success to 22.7\%, closing one specific hole without proving general robustness.

