\section{The real-world theme: claims, reimbursement and the cost of getting it wrong}
Healthcare insurers process millions of claims every year. Each claim is a request to reimburse a service delivered to a patient by a hospital, clinic, physician or pharmacy. Reimbursement is governed by insurance policies, tariffs and contracts that define what is covered and at what price \cite{dafny2005,ma2002,diwan2026}. Deciding whether to pay a claim, and how much, sounds simple, but in practice it is a hard task for three reasons.

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Volume.} The data used in this internship alone contains more than one million raw claim rows from four utilisation workbooks, covering the years 2013 to 2016.
  \item \textbf{Ambiguity.} Most claims correspond to legitimate care, but some involve fraud, abuse, billing errors, unnecessary services or practices such as upcoding, phantom billing, duplicate claims and unbundling \cite{leder2025,berwick2012,ai2018,ai2022}. From the claim alone, these are often hard to tell apart from unusual but legitimate medicine.
  \item \textbf{Incentives.} Providers respond to the way they are paid. Dafny \cite{dafny2005} shows that hospitals change their diagnosis coding when reimbursement prices change, without a change in patients' needs. Suspicious patterns therefore have to be read in context, not automatically labelled as fraud.
\end{enumerate}

Fraud, waste and abuse (FWA) cause unnecessary expenditure, inefficient use of resources and loss of trust between insurers, providers and patients \cite{leder2025,berwick2012}.

\begin{plainwords}
Think of the insurer as a shop that pays thousands of bills a day. A few bills are inflated or wrong. If the shop only checks \emph{after} paying, it must chase people to get money back. It would be better to spot the suspicious bills \emph{before} paying, and better still to test, in a safe setting, how a clever cheat would try to slip bills through.
\end{plainwords}

\section{The situation at Al Hilal Takaful: detection after payment}
In the data provided, problematic claims are identified \emph{reactively}. A claim is paid. Later, a reviewer finds a problem, and the claim is recorded in the \emph{Recovery} sheet together with a recovery or ex-gratia amount. The insurer then tries to recover the money. This process has three drawbacks.

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{The money has already left.} Recovery means claiming back money that has already been paid.
  \item \textbf{Review capacity is not guided.} Without a risk ranking, reviewers cannot focus on the claims most likely to be problematic.
  \item \textbf{The evidence is used only retrospectively.} The set of confirmed recoveries is a record of what has already been caught. A model trained on it learns past patterns and says little about strategies nobody has tried yet.
\end{itemize}

Only 614 of the 839{,}209 usable claims (0.073\%) carry a recovery amount. This extreme rarity shapes every modelling decision in the report (Chapter~\ref{ch:concepts}).

