\subsection{What the papers teach}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Context first.} An unusual bill is a hypothesis, not a verdict: payment rules shape provider behaviour \cite{leder2025,dafny2005,ma2002,diwan2026}.
  \item \textbf{Fraud is one part of waste.} Detection must be weighed against its operational cost \cite{berwick2012,banulescu2025}.
  \item \textbf{Evidence is hard to compare.} Data, labels and metrics differ between studies, so everything must be documented \cite{ai2022,dupreez2025}.
  \item \textbf{Labels are scarce and incomplete.} This calls for imbalance-aware learning and complementary unsupervised signals \cite{debener2023,dupreez2025}.
  \item \textbf{History does not anticipate adaptation.} Detectors trained on past fraud need adversarial testing \cite{lunghi2025,pang2025,beling2021}.
\end{itemize}


\section{Papers on Methods}
Besides the literature on fraud, the papers behind each tool were read, so that every method is used as its authors intend.

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Gradient boosting:} XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost}, for the defender.
  \item \textbf{Synthetic tabular data:} CTGAN \cite{xu2019ctgan}, for the synthetic claims.
  \item \textbf{Reinforcement learning:} PPO \cite{schulman2017ppo}, Stable-Baselines3 \cite{raffin2021sb3} and Gymnasium \cite{towers2024gymnasium}, for the attacker and its environment.
