\section{Imbalanced Classification and Metrics}\label{sec:metrics}

\subsection{The accuracy trap}
Only 614 of 839{,}209 usable claims are positive. A ``model'' that always answers ``not recovered'' is right for 838{,}595 claims:
\[
\text{Accuracy} = \frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN} = \frac{838{,}595}{839{,}209} \approx 99.93\%,
\]
and it finds \emph{no} recovery. Accuracy is therefore useless here.

\begin{plainwords}
If only one ticket in a thousand is a winner, a person who says ``no ticket wins'' is right 99.9\% of the time and completely useless. What matters is how many winners are found, and at what cost in tickets checked.
\end{plainwords}

\subsection{Metrics used}
With $TP$, $FP$, $FN$ the true positives, false positives and false negatives at a threshold,
\[
\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP},\qquad
\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}.
\]
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{PR-AUC} summarises the precision--recall curve. A random scorer has PR-AUC equal to the positive rate (here below 0.001), so even a PR-AUC of 0.03 is far above chance. For rare positives, PR-AUC is more informative than ROC-AUC \cite{saito2015}.
  \item \textbf{ROC-AUC} is the probability that a random positive receives a higher score than a random negative. It can look good even when precision is very low, which is why it is reported only as a secondary measure.
  \item \textbf{Recall@k\%} is the share of all positives that fall in the top $k$\% of claims by score. It corresponds to a reviewer who can only open $k$\% of the claims. The \textbf{lift} is $\text{Recall@}k\%\,/\,(k/100)$. For example, Recall@5\% of 55.5\% means a lift of about $11.1\times$ over random review.
\end{itemize}

\section{Weak Labels and Label Maturity}
The label is ``had a recovery''. A claim with no recovery may be legitimate or simply never reviewed, so the negative class is \emph{unlabelled} rather than clean. This is known as a weak-label or positive--unlabelled setting \cite{ai2022,dupreez2025}. Two consequences follow: measured precision is probably a lower bound of the true precision, and models learn what reviewers historically caught, including their habits.

A second effect is \emph{label maturity}: a recent claim may not yet have been reviewed, so its recovery rate looks lower than it will eventually be. This is relevant for the last months of the data (Section~\ref{sec:eda-monthly}).

\section{Data Leakage and Temporal Validation}
\begin{description}[leftmargin=0.9cm,style=nextline]
  \item[Target leakage.] Using a column that is only known after the outcome (for example \texttt{RECOVERYTYPE}). The model then reads the answer instead of learning risk. Section~\ref{sec:leakage} lists the 14 such columns excluded.
  \item[Train--test contamination.] Information from the test period reaching the training pipeline, for example statistics computed on the whole dataset. This report keeps one such simplification and states it (Chapter~\ref{ch:limits}).
  \item[Temporal split.] Claims are split by year, not randomly: train on earlier years and test on later ones. This imitates deployment, where the model scores future claims.
\end{description}

\section{Gradient Boosting and XGBoost}
Gradient boosting builds a prediction as a sum of many small decision trees, each correcting the errors of the previous ones:
\[
F_m(x) = F_{m-1}(x) + \eta\, h_m(x),
\]
where $h_m$ is the new tree and $\eta$ the learning rate. XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost} is an efficient implementation, used here for the defender. It handles mixed numeric and categorical inputs and non-linear interactions, which suits claim data. Class imbalance is handled with a weight on positive examples:
\[
\texttt{scale\_pos\_weight}=\frac{N_{\text{negative}}}{N_{\text{positive}}}.
\]

\begin{plainwords}
Imagine a team of beginners. The first guesses roughly; the second only studies what the first got wrong; the third studies what is still wrong, and so on. Together they become a strong predictor. Giving rare positives a heavy weight tells the team that missing a recovery is much more costly than a false alarm.
\end{plainwords}

\section{Peer-Group Deviation}
A claim is judged against similar claims. For claim $i$ with amount $a_i$ in peer group $g(i)$ (here: ICD chapter $\times$ age band $\times$ service category),
\[
\texttt{PEER\_ZSCORE}_i=\frac{a_i-\mu_{g(i)}}{\sigma_{g(i)}},\qquad
\texttt{AMOUNT\_OVER\_MEDIAN}_i=\frac{a_i}{\operatorname{median}_{g(i)}(a)}.
\]
If similar claims usually cost AED 1{,}000 and a claim costs AED 5{,}000, the second score is 5. It is a tariff-free proxy for ``medically justified cost''. These scores are built in the data pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:features}) and, as Chapter~\ref{ch:selfplay} suggests, their absence is the likely main weakness of the first defender (to be tested).

\section{Synthetic Tabular Data: GANs and CTGAN}
A generative adversarial network (GAN) trains two networks against each other: a generator $G$ that creates fake rows and a discriminator $D$ that tries to tell them from real rows:
\[
\min_G \max_D \; \mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}}}[\log D(x)] + \mathbb{E}_{z\sim p_z}[\log(1-D(G(z)))].
\]
CTGAN \cite{xu2019ctgan} adapts this to tables with mixed columns. It normalises each continuous column with a mode-specific transformation (suited to skewed amounts), conditions the generator on a discrete column, and uses \emph{training-by-sampling}: rare category values are deliberately oversampled during training so that the generator learns them. A known side effect, observed here, is that unconditioned sampling reproduces this oversampled distribution (Section~\ref{sec:ctgan}).

\section{Reinforcement Learning and PPO}
Reinforcement learning (RL) models an agent that acts in an environment to maximise a reward. It is formalised as a Markov decision process $(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,R,\gamma)$: at each step the agent observes a state $s_t$, chooses an action $a_t$ from a policy $\pi_\theta(a\mid s)$, and receives a reward $r_t$. Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) \cite{schulman2017ppo} improves the policy with a clipped objective that prevents too-large updates:
\[
L(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_t\Big[\min\big(\rho_t(\theta)\hat A_t,\ \operatorname{clip}(\rho_t(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\hat A_t\big)\Big],\quad
\rho_t(\theta)=\frac{\pi_\theta(a_t\mid s_t)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(a_t\mid s_t)},
\]
where $\hat A_t$ is the estimated advantage. The implementation of Stable-Baselines3 \cite{raffin2021sb3} is used with environments written for Gymnasium \cite{towers2024gymnasium}.

\section{Red-Teaming, Self-Play and Co-Evolution}
\emph{Red-teaming} means attacking your own system to find its weaknesses before an enemy does. In \emph{self-play}, two agents are trained against each other. In the full two-sided game, with $x$ a claim, $\pi_F$ and $\pi_D$ the fraudster and defender policies and $d(x)\in[0,1]$ the detection decision, a simplified pair of objectives is
\[
\max_{\pi_F}\ \mathbb{E}\big[\text{payout}(x)\,(1-d(x)) - c_F\,\text{effort}(x)\big],\qquad
\max_{\pi_D}\ \mathbb{E}\big[\text{caught}(x) - c_D\,\text{false\_alarm}(x)\big].
\]
Training both in alternation is called \emph{co-evolution}. This report builds the one-sided first step: a learning fraudster against a \emph{static} defender, followed by one defender update.

\begin{plainwords}
A locksmith who tests the locks of a bank by trying to break in is red-teaming. If the locksmith finds a way in, the bank changes the lock and the locksmith tries again. The MVP does this once: one break-in attempt, one new lock, and a second attempt with the same technique to see whether the new lock holds.
\end{plainwords}
\begin{chaptersummary}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item Accuracy is meaningless at 0.073\% positives (99.93\% for a useless model); use PR-AUC and Recall@k.
  \item Only information known at submission may be used as input; split by time.
  \item Peer-group deviation is a tariff-free proxy for ``justified cost''.
  \item Self-play is a locksmith testing the lock: attack, patch, attack again.
\end{itemize}
\end{chaptersummary}

