\section{What This Means for the Solution}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Read alerts in context}, for example by comparing a claim with similar claims, never as proof of fraud.
  \item \textbf{Detect earlier}: issues are found only after payment, so the objective is to act before or at payment.
  \item \textbf{Measure the workload}, not only accuracy: use a review budget (the riskiest 5\% of claims) and recall@k.
  \item \textbf{Anticipate adaptation}: a detector built on past, caught fraud says little about strategies nobody has tried yet, which is what the self-play tests.
\end{itemize}

\begin{chaptersummary}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item FWA come from payment incentives, imperfect rates, moral hazard and weak monitoring.
  \item An unusual bill is a hypothesis, not a verdict, because providers respond to payment rules.
  \item The problem is detection after payment: improper claims are found only after the money has left, and then recovery is needed.
\end{itemize}
\end{chaptersummary}

