\section{Solution Concept}
A model trained on confirmed recoveries only knows fraud that has already happened. The solution builds a closed loop in which a simulated fraudster \emph{invents} evasion strategies against a detector. The strategies that succeed are labelled as fraud and folded back into training, which patches the holes before a real actor exploits them. The output is a red-team report, not an operational fraud-generation system.

\begin{plainwords}
Three actors are involved: a \emph{guard} (the detector), a \emph{thief} (the attacker agent) and a \emph{training ground} (synthetic claims in a closed environment). The thief practises on the training ground. Every trick that works is shown to the guard, who learns to recognise it. Then the thief tries the same trick again, to see whether the guard has really learned.
\end{plainwords}

