\section{Scope Agreed Before Building the MVP}
The full proposal describes a PettingZoo multi-agent environment, an attacker that keeps adapting over thousands of rounds, and a defender that co-evolves round by round. That is closer to a thesis chapter than to a notebook extension. Three scope questions were therefore settled explicitly before building.

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{MVP first:} a single PPO fraudster (Gymnasium, not PettingZoo) against one \textbf{static} defender, not the full co-evolution loop.
  \item \textbf{Real-data boundary:} CTGAN trains on real claims; everything downstream in the RL sandbox sees only CTGAN-sampled synthetic claims.
  \item \textbf{New dependencies:} \texttt{torch} (CPU-only build, since the available GPU driver, CUDA 11.2, is old), \texttt{gymnasium}, \texttt{stable-baselines3} and \texttt{ctgan} in \texttt{scripts/venv}.
\end{enumerate}

One bonus beyond this scope was added: a single round of defender retraining (Section~\ref{sec:retrain}). It was cheap to run and far more informative than stopping at the static comparison.

