\section{Inputs}
Nine Excel workbooks: four utilisation workbooks (claims) and five beneficiary workbooks (policy and member data), as listed in Chapter~\ref{ch:datainv}. The exports are not clean tables: each sheet has a handful of title rows above the real header, some workbooks carry a corrupted defined name that crashes \texttt{openpyxl}, and several reference sheets have no tabular header at all.

\section{Step 1: Loading and Consolidation}

\subsection{\texttt{strip\_defined\_names}}
Some workbooks have a broken named-range definition that crashes \texttt{openpyxl}. The fix creates a temporary copy of the file with the defined-names XML entry stripped. The workbook is not re-exported, so the data stays untouched.

\subsection{\texttt{detect\_header\_row}}
It scans the first rows of a sheet for the row that really looks like column headers (not a title or logo row), because raw exports put three to four decorative rows above the header.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Header detection (illustrative).}]
def detect_header_row(raw, key_cols, scan_rows=15):
    """Return the index of the first row that contains known column names."""
    for i, row in raw.head(scan_rows).iterrows():
        values = set(row.astype(str).str.strip())
        if any(k in values for k in key_cols):
            return i
    return None   # no tabular header: reference sheet, skipped and logged
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{\texttt{load\_workbook\_sheets}}
It opens every \texttt{.xlsx} of a folder, loads every sheet whose header can be detected and skips the others (logged as warnings: these are reference or cover sheets, not data loss). Every row is tagged with its origin: \texttt{source\_file} and \texttt{REPORT\_TYPE} for claims, \texttt{BENEF\_SHEET} for beneficiaries.

