\section{Step 2: Cleaning and Standardisation}

\subsection{The join key problem}
\texttt{BENEFICIARYIDENTIFIER} (claims side) and \texttt{BENEFICIARYID} (beneficiary side) look like a matching pair but belong to two different identifier systems: a short hexadecimal code on one side, a sequential numeric ID on the other. They never overlap, so joining on them \emph{silently} matches 0 rows. The reliable shared key is the \textbf{beneficiary full name + date of birth}, built the same way on both sides.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Join key on name and date of birth (illustrative).}]
def make_key(df, name_col, dob_col):
    name = df[name_col].astype(str).str.strip().str.upper()
    dob  = pd.to_datetime(df[dob_col], errors="coerce").dt.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
    return name + "|" + dob

claims["JOIN_KEY"] = make_key(claims, "BENEFICIARY", "DOB")
benef["JOIN_KEY"]  = make_key(benef,  "BENEFICIARY", "DOB")
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{caution}[Lesson]
A join that returns no error can still return no match. The mismatch was found by checking the number of matched rows, not by trusting the column names.
\end{caution}

\subsection{Dates and financial columns}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Dates} are parsed to real datetimes.
  \item \textbf{Financial columns} (\texttt{PAYERAMOUNTCLAIMED}, \texttt{BENEFICIARYAMOUNTCLAIMED}, \texttt{TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED}, \texttt{TOTALPAYABLE}, \texttt{PAYERSHARE}, \texttt{BENEFICIARYSHAREAPPROVED}, \texttt{DEDUCTIONAMOUNT}, \dots) are coerced to numeric, and a \texttt{\_WAS\_MISSING} flag is recorded \emph{before} blanks are filled with 0.0. A genuine AED 0 claim therefore stays distinguishable from a blank or unparseable source cell.
\end{itemize}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Numeric coercion with a missing flag (illustrative).}]
for c in financial_cols:
    s = pd.to_numeric(df[c], errors="coerce")
    df[c + "_WAS_MISSING"] = s.isna().astype(int)   # record BEFORE filling
    df[c] = s.fillna(0.0)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Step 3: Business-Logic Filters}

\subsection{A. Status definitions}
Each \texttt{STATUS} code is mapped to a plain-English definition (17 statuses, from \texttt{Initial} and \texttt{Authorized} to \texttt{Settled}, \texttt{Rejected} and \texttt{Void}).

\subsection{B. Rejected and void claims}
These are split into their own frame (\texttt{df\_excluded\_claims}) and exported separately instead of being silently dropped: they carry no recovery information and are not valid training rows, but they stay on disk for audit. In this data pull, 0 rows were Rejected or Void.

\subsection{C. Beneficiary merge}
A left join on name and date of birth matched \textbf{944{,}205 of 1{,}017{,}993 claims (92.8\%)} to a beneficiary record. Sixteen duplicate columns with the suffix \texttt{\_beneficiary} were dropped (the same fact is already on the claims side). Three sparse columns with $\geq$97\% missing values were dropped: \texttt{REINSURER} (only one source file ever populates it: a real gap in the source, not a bug), \texttt{Recovery Notes} and \texttt{RecoveryNotes}.

\subsection{D. Post-termination claims}
Nine rows whose service date falls after the policy's expiry date (\texttt{TXNDATE > EXPIRYDATE}) were \textbf{removed}, not only flagged: a claim serviced outside the covered period is not a valid case for a recovery model to learn from. They are exported to \texttt{excluded\_post\_termination\_claims.csv} for audit.

\subsection{E. Missing core fields}
Rows missing any of \texttt{TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED}, \texttt{TXNDATE}, \texttt{STATUS}, \texttt{PROVIDERTYPE}, \texttt{CLAIMID} or \texttt{ICD CHAPTER} were dropped: \textbf{178{,}775 rows, 17.6\%} of the pre-cleaning total. This is the largest single reduction of the pipeline. These rows are unusable for modelling whatever the imputation strategy.

\subsection{F. Recovery sub-table}
Rows with \texttt{RECOVERY/EX-GRATIA > 0} are pulled into their own frame (\texttt{df\_recovery}), the basis of the \texttt{RECOVERY\_FLAG} label.

