\vspace{0.6em}


\subsection{Peer-deviation features}
Claims are grouped by \texttt{ICD CHAPTER} $\times$ \texttt{AGEBAND} $\times$ \texttt{FOB}. Within each group, the median, mean and standard deviation of the claimed amount are computed, then \texttt{PEER\_ZSCORE} and \texttt{AMOUNT\_OVER\_MEDIAN} (formulas in Chapter~\ref{ch:concepts}). If similar claims usually cost AED 1{,}000 and a claim costs AED 5{,}000, \texttt{AMOUNT\_OVER\_MEDIAN} is 5. This is a tariff-free version of ``medically justified cost''. They are part of \texttt{claims\_ml\_ready.csv}; the self-play defender leaves them out on purpose (Section~\ref{sec:defv1}).

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Peer-deviation features (illustrative).}]
keys = ["ICD CHAPTER", "AGEBAND", "FOB"]
g = df.groupby(keys)["TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED"]
df["PEER_MEDIAN"] = g.transform("median")
df["PEER_MEAN"]   = g.transform("mean")
df["PEER_STD"]    = g.transform("std")
df["PEER_ZSCORE"] = (df["TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED"] - df["PEER_MEAN"]) / df["PEER_STD"]
df["AMOUNT_OVER_MEDIAN"] = df["TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED"] / df["PEER_MEDIAN"]
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{plainwords}
Comparing a claim with similar claims is like judging a restaurant bill: AED 200 is normal for a family dinner but strange for a single coffee. The peer group tells the model what ``similar'' costs.
\end{plainwords}

\section{Step 8: Encoding, Missing Values and Scaling}

\subsection{Encoding}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{One-hot encoding} for 18 low-cardinality columns (for example \texttt{GENDER}, \texttt{PROVIDERTYPE}, \texttt{FOB}, \texttt{CHRONIC}, \texttt{CLAIM CATEGORY}, \texttt{NETWORK TYPE}, \texttt{PROVIDER EMIRATE}), giving about 75 dummy columns. One zero-variance dummy was dropped (\texttt{CURRENCY\_Arab Emirates Dirham}: every claim has the same currency).
  \item \textbf{Frequency encoding} for 10 high-cardinality columns: \texttt{PROVIDER}, \texttt{NATIONALITY}, \texttt{ICD CHAPTER}, \texttt{ICD SUB CHAPTER}, \texttt{NETWORK}, \texttt{EMPNO}, \texttt{CATEGORY}, \texttt{PROVIDERCOUNTRY}, \texttt{PROVIDER GROUP}, \texttt{PRODUCT}. One-hot encoding thousands of providers would explode the width and memorise identities instead of learning behaviour.
  \item \textbf{Nine raw datetime columns dropped} (\texttt{EFFECTIVEDATE}, \texttt{EXPIRYDATE}, \texttt{DOB}, \texttt{TXNDATE}, \texttt{DISCHARGEDATE}, \texttt{MEMBER START DATE}, \texttt{RECEPTIONDATE}, \texttt{CONTRACTEFFDATE}, \texttt{CONTRACTEXPDATE}): their signal is already in the engineered \texttt{*\_DAYS} and \texttt{CLAIM\_MONTH} features.
\end{itemize}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Frequency encoding (illustrative).}]
for c in high_card_cols:
    df[c] = df[c].map(df[c].value_counts(normalize=True))
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{caution}[Known limitation, stated in the notebook itself]
Frequency encoding is computed on the \emph{whole} dataset, not fitted on the training fold only. In a strict train--test regime this leaks a small amount of test-period information into the encoding. It is kept as a stated simplification and named in the limitations (Chapter~\ref{ch:limits}).
\end{caution}

\subsection{Missing values}
Remaining numeric missing values are filled with 0, and indicator columns (for example \texttt{LOS\_WAS\_NA}, \texttt{NETPREMIUM\_WAS\_NA}, \texttt{GROSSPREMIUM\_WAS\_NA}) let the model separate a true zero from a missing value.

\subsection{Scaling}
Thirty-three continuous numeric columns (including \texttt{AGE}, \texttt{LOS}, all claimed-amount columns, all engineered day-gap features and all frequency and peer-deviation features) are standardised with \texttt{StandardScaler} (mean 0, standard deviation 1). One-hot dummies and 0/1 flags stay unscaled: they are already on a comparable range, and standardising them would roughly double memory use for no benefit.

\texttt{CLAIM\_YEAR} is \textbf{deliberately kept raw}. The train/validation/test split is built directly on this column, and a standardised z-score would destroy the real years (2013--2016) needed for the split.

