The eight engineered columns are safe to freeze per baseline claim, because the agent never controls timing, provider identity or resubmission in this MVP: it controls only the claimed amount.

\subsection{\texttt{encode\_amount\_only()}}
Given an already-encoded baseline row, it recomputes \emph{only} the two amount-derived entries (\texttt{TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED\_scaled} and \texttt{CLAIM\_AMOUNT\_LOG\_scaled}) for a new claimed amount. Demographics, provider, diagnosis and timing stay frozen. This is what makes per-step re-scoring cheap enough for RL.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Re-scoring a new amount (illustrative).}]
def encode_amount_only(encoded_row, amount, scaler):
    row = encoded_row.copy()
    row["TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED_scaled"] = (amount - scaler.mean_amt) / scaler.std_amt
    log_amt = np.log1p(amount)
    row["CLAIM_AMOUNT_LOG_scaled"] = (log_amt - scaler.mean_log) / scaler.std_log
    return row
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{caution}[Deliberate exclusion, and its consequence]
\texttt{PEER\_ZSCORE}, \texttt{AMOUNT\_OVER\_MEDIAN} and \texttt{PROVIDER\_AVG\_AMOUNT}, the amount-derived features built in the data pipeline, are \emph{not} in this feature set. They depend on peer or provider group statistics that would themselves have to shift as the agent changes its claimed amount, which the per-step function does not handle. This exclusion is the most likely cause of the main finding of Chapter~\ref{ch:results}; it is stated here so that it is not a surprise later.
\end{caution}

