\paragraph{How to read these numbers.} A random scorer has a PR-AUC equal to the positive rate (below 0.001), so 0.0339 is far above chance. A Recall@5\% of 55.5\% means that reviewing the riskiest 5\% of claims finds 55.5\% of the recoveries, a lift of about $11\times$ over random review.

\paragraph{Why the defender is weak.} The reduced feature set lacks the peer-deviation and provider-amount features, the ones that judge an amount against similar claims. This was checked during development, in a run that is not part of the final notebook: a first attempt with only the raw columns (no timing or provider-history features) scored a PR-AUC of 0.0040; adding the eight amount-independent engineered columns from the data pipeline brought it to 0.0339. The engineered features carry real signal, so the pipeline works; what the defender lacks is an explicit feature that compares an amount with similar claims. A tree model could in principle learn such interactions from amount, claim category and diagnosis chapter, but it has no direct signal for them.

\paragraph{Detection threshold.} The threshold (0.0117) is the 95th percentile of the defender's own scores on its training data. A claim is ``caught'' if it would fall in the riskiest 5\% by this defender's own judgement, mirroring an audit team that can review only the riskiest 5\% of claims.

