\subsection{A known quirk: the synthetic recovery rate}
The synthetic \texttt{RECOVERY\_FLAG} rate is \textbf{6.48\%}, far above the real training rate of 0.084\%. This is CTGAN's documented training-by-sampling behaviour \cite{xu2019ctgan}: rare discrete values are intentionally oversampled during training, and unconditioned \texttt{.sample()} reproduces that skewed distribution unless a condition vector is supplied. \textbf{This does not distort anything downstream}: the flag is metadata carried through the CSV and never read by \texttt{ClaimFraudEnv} as a feature. The environment only uses a synthetic claim's demographic, provider and amount fields as a template to build a fraud attempt on.

\section{Step 4: The Environment \texttt{ClaimFraudEnv}}

\subsection{Formulation}
Each \textbf{episode} is one synthetic baseline claim drawn from the 20{,}000-row pool. The agent has a \textbf{fraud budget} equal to three times the claim's legitimate amount and at most five submissions to extract it. In plain words: the thief wants to take as much of the budget as possible without being caught.

