\vspace{0.6em}


\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Step logic (illustrative reconstruction of the documented behaviour).}]
def step(action):
    frac   = (action + 1.0) / 2.0                 # rescale [-1, 1] -> [0, 1]
    amount = frac * budget_remaining              # fraction of remaining budget
    row    = encode_amount_only(baseline_row, amount)
    score  = defender.predict_proba(row)[1]
    steps_left -= 1
    if score >= threshold:                        # caught
        caught = True
        budget_remaining = 0.0                    # remaining budget forfeited (earlier rewards kept)
        done = True
    else:                                         # evaded
        amount_kept      += amount                # separate accumulator (see bug fix)
        budget_remaining -= amount
        done = (budget_remaining <= 0) or (steps_left == 0)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Reward function}\label{sec:reward}
The reward is defined with the following quantities.

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item $B = 3.0 \times \texttt{TOTALAMOUNTCLAIMED}$ is the total fraud budget (\texttt{FRAUD\_BUDGET\_MULTIPLIER} = 3.0).
  \item $R_t$ is the budget still left \emph{before} step $t$; it starts at $R_1 = B$.
  \item The agent submits $a_t = \text{frac}\times R_t$, where $\text{frac} = \operatorname{clip}\big((\text{action}+1)/2,\,0,\,1\big)$.
  \item The agent is \textbf{caught} when the defender's \texttt{predict\_proba} is greater than or equal to the detection threshold.
\end{itemize}

The reward at each step is
\[
r_t=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{a_t}{B} & \text{if not caught (the amount is banked)},\quad R_{t+1}=R_t-a_t,\\[2ex]
-\,1.5\,\dfrac{R_t}{B} & \text{if caught, the episode ends and } R \text{ is set to } 0.
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{center}
\small
\captionof{table}{Reward of one step of \texttt{ClaimFraudEnv}.}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l l@{}}
\toprule
\rowcolor{blue!14} \textbf{Outcome} & \textbf{Reward} & \textbf{Effect} \\
\midrule
Not caught (banked) & $r_t = a_t / B$ & $R_{t+1} = R_t - a_t$ and the episode continues \\
Caught & $r_t = -1.5\,R_t / B$ & The episode ends and $R$ is set to 0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Banked reward.} Each banked currency unit is worth $1/B$. If the agent banks the whole budget, the episode return is exactly $+1.0$.
  \item \textbf{Detection penalty.} \texttt{CAUGHT\_PENALTY\_SCALE} = 1.5 multiplies the remaining budget as a fraction of $B$. Because $R_t$ is read \emph{before} the flagged claim is subtracted, the flagged sub-claim is part of the penalty. The penalty ranges from $-1.5$ (caught at the first step, when $R_t=B$) to nearly 0 (caught after almost everything has been banked).
  \item \textbf{Termination.} An episode stops (\emph{terminated}) when the agent is caught. It is \emph{truncated} after \texttt{MAX\_STEPS} = 5 steps or once $R \leq 10^{-6}$.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Worked example.} If the agent banks 60\% of $B$ and is then caught with 40\% of $B$ remaining, its episode return is $0.6 - 1.5\times 0.4 = 0.0$.

\begin{caution}[Getting caught is not a total loss]
Rewards banked at earlier steps are \emph{not} taken back when the agent is caught. Getting caught forfeits the \emph{remaining} budget and adds the penalty above, so the return can be zero, or negative if the agent is caught early, but it is not automatically a total loss. A code comment in the environment states that ``getting caught loses everything''; it overstates the behaviour, and this report uses the formula above.
\end{caution}

\subsection{Strategies are not hard-coded}
Two example tricks named in the proposal, \emph{splitting} a claim into several smaller ones and \emph{staying under an alert threshold}, are not coded into the environment. They can \emph{emerge} from the amount-fraction policy that the agent learns (Section~\ref{sec:res-strategy}).

\subsection{Metrics}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Attack success rate:} share of evaluation episodes in which the agent is not caught (tracked through the \texttt{caught} flag).
  \item \textbf{Mean fraud budget kept:} average fraction of the initial fraud budget that the agent banks (\texttt{kept\_fraction}).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{A bug found and fixed during development}
The \texttt{kept\_fraction} metric was first computed from \texttt{budget\_remaining}, which the step logic sets to zero on capture for internal bookkeeping. As a result, every \emph{caught} episode incorrectly reported \texttt{kept\_fraction = 1.0}, as if nothing had been lost, although getting caught means forfeiting the rest. The fix introduced a separate \texttt{amount\_kept} accumulator that increases only on non-caught steps. \textbf{The bug never affected the attack success rate}, which is tracked independently through the \texttt{caught} flag. This is why the two training runs before and after the fix show identical success-rate curves but different kept-fraction curves, and how the bug was detected.

