\subsection{What the curves show}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Attack success} stays between about 89\% and 92\% from the first checkpoint to the last, with one spike to 96\% at 60{,}000 steps, and \emph{always above} the random baseline of 86\%. There is no upward trend: the curve is essentially flat from the first checkpoint onwards.
  \item \textbf{Budget kept} fluctuates between about 83\% and 90\%. It falls \emph{below} the random baseline of 84.6\% at three checkpoints (50{,}000, 70{,}000 and 80{,}000 steps, lowest 82.6\%), so the average amount extracted is not reliably improved by learning.
  \item The final evaluation gives 90.3\% attack success, i.e.\ 271 of 300 episodes not caught, and 88.2\% mean budget kept.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Reading the result correctly}
\begin{keyresult}[The most important honest finding of the MVP]
A \emph{random} policy already wins 86\% of the time against \texttt{defender\_v1}. This is not evidence that real insurance fraud is trivially easy. It is most likely explained by the decision to exclude the peer-deviation and provider-amount features from this defender.
\end{keyresult}

Without those features, \texttt{defender\_v1} has no direct way to judge whether a claimed amount is abnormal \emph{relative to what similar claims usually cost}. It only sees whether the raw claim looks unusual against the whole population's demographic and timing mix, and a single claim at three times the legitimate amount probably does not look unusual on those terms alone. Because a random policy already wins most of the time, there is little room for learning to improve on: most of the achievable value was captured by chance. The practical conclusion is that \emph{a weak defender is vulnerable not only to a sophisticated learned attack but to almost anything}, which argues for closing the suspected blind spot before building a more elaborate attacker.

\begin{plainwords}
If a burglar who tries doors at random gets in 86 times out of 100, the problem is the lock, not the burglar. Training a cleverer burglar then adds only a few more break-ins.
\end{plainwords}

\subsection{A note on statistical uncertainty}
Each checkpoint uses 300 episodes. With a success rate near 90\%, the standard error is roughly $\sqrt{0.9\times0.1/300}\approx1.7$ percentage points, so the fluctuations between 89\% and 92\% are within noise, and only the 96\% spike stands out. The random baseline was also measured on 300 episodes, so the 4.3-point gap between 86.0\% and 90.3\% is about 1.6 standard errors: suggestive but not conclusive. The result should therefore be read as ``PPO is at least as good as random, with a possible modest gain'', not as a demonstrated improvement. Repeating the evaluation with more episodes and several random seeds is listed in the future work.

