\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{The dominant strategy (90\%) is a single, large, one-shot claim for the entire 3$\times$ fraud budget, not splitting.} This is consistent with the diagnosis above: if \texttt{defender\_v1} cannot tell an inflated amount from a normal one without peer-group context, the agent does not need to disguise its claims as several small ones. Brute-force amount inflation already works most of the time.
  \item \textbf{A minority (about 10\%) splits} the budget across two to five sub-claims. These are presumably the harder baseline claims, with individually riskier demographic, provider or timing combinations, where one claim at 3$\times$ would be too conspicuous and splitting genuinely helps. This explanation is a plausible reading, not a tested fact.
\end{itemize}

\begin{keyresult}[Red-team takeaway]
Undetected amount inflation is the \emph{primary} exposure of this defender. Splitting is a \emph{secondary} strategy to which the agent falls back only when the primary one does not work. A report that warned only about splitting, the literal example in the proposal, would have missed the bigger and more common hole.
\end{keyresult}

