\subsection{The mechanism, stated without overselling}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \texttt{defender\_v2} did \emph{not} become much smarter about fraud in general. Its real-data PR-AUC moved only from 0.034 to 0.049, and its ROC-AUC \emph{decreased} from 0.832 to 0.781, which suggests that the retraining moved the decision boundary toward the amount ranges resembling this attacker's submissions, at a small cost in broader generalisation.
  \item What happened is narrower and more mechanical: the defender learned that ``claimed amounts in the range this attacker was using are suspicious'', which closes exactly the hole this attacker had found. The change of the detection threshold (0.0117 to 0.3030) reflects the very different score distribution of the retrained model.
  \item \textbf{The promise:} discovered holes close quickly and cheaply, with one round, 337 examples and a few minutes of work.
  \item \textbf{The limit:} the evaluation attacker is the \emph{same} attacker whose examples trained \texttt{defender\_v2}, so the large drop is partly expected. One round does not generalise to holes not yet discovered. The real test is a \emph{fresh or re-adapted} attacker against \texttt{defender\_v2}, which is exactly the argument for many rounds and an attacker that keeps adapting.
\end{itemize}

