\section{Answers to the Guiding Questions Tested Experimentally}
\paragraph{GQ7 -- Do detectors keep working when fraudsters adapt?} In this setting, no: the first defender is beaten 90.3\% of the time, even though it reaches Recall@5\% of 55.5\% on real data. A good score on historical data does not guarantee robustness against adaptation.

\paragraph{GQ10 -- Can attacks be explored without exposing patient data?} Yes: with a closed environment fed only by CTGAN claims (20{,}000 rows), the whole attack and retraining loop ran without any real claim entering the sandbox.

\paragraph{GQ11 -- How weak is our detector against an adaptive attacker?} Very weak when it lacks peer-group amount context: a random attacker already succeeds in 86\% of episodes. After one retraining round, the attack success of the same attacker is 22.7\%.

\paragraph{Challenge.} The challenge asked whether an AI could invent new fraud strategies against our own defences so that holes get patched before a real person finds them. The MVP shows that the mechanism works end to end: the agent found a dominant strategy (amount inflation) and a secondary one (splitting), and patching with the discovered examples reduced that attacker's success sharply. It does \emph{not} show that the patched defender is robust to new strategies.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Three readings of the result}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Ceiling effect.} Because chance already wins 86\%, there is little room for any learning algorithm to show a gain. Comparing with a random policy is therefore essential, and the report keeps it in every comparison.
  \item \textbf{Detector design.} The main weakness is a design decision (excluded peer features), documented in advance. The experiment is consistent with its expected consequence, but does not isolate it: a direct test would re-run the same attack against a defender \emph{with} the peer-deviation features. This is the first item of the future work. Until that test is run, the claim that peer features would block a 3$\times$ inflation remains a hypothesis.
  \item \textbf{Operating point.} ``Caught'' means being in the riskiest 5\% of the defender's training scores. A different review budget (for example 10\%) would change every attack success rate.
\end{enumerate}

