  \item This closes one known hole; it does not prove general robustness.
\end{itemize}
\end{chaptersummary}

\chapter{Limitations}\label{ch:limits}

\begin{chapteroverview}
This chapter covers:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item limitations of labels and evidence, of the data pipeline, of the self-play MVP and of the synthetic data
\end{itemize}
\end{chapteroverview}


\section{Labels and Evidence}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Weak labels.} ``Not recovered'' does not mean ``clean''; it may mean nobody has reviewed the claim. Negative labels are noisy, measured precision is probably a lower bound of true precision, and models learn what reviewers historically caught \cite{ai2022,dupreez2025}.
  \item \textbf{Label maturity.} Recent claims have had less time to be reviewed, so the recovery rate of the last months (and of the test period) may be understated.
  \item \textbf{Extreme imbalance.} With 614 positives (252 in the training years), metrics have high variance; a single positive more or less changes a rate noticeably.
  \item \textbf{Evidence base.} The literature has limited comparability and is concentrated on U.S.\ data \cite{ai2022,dupreez2025}.\end{itemize}

\section{Data and Preprocessing}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Frequency encoding on the whole dataset} leaks a small amount of test-period information into the encoding. It is kept as a stated simplification; the fix is to fit encodings on the training fold only.
  \item \textbf{17.6\% of rows} were dropped for missing core fields: the largest single reduction. If those rows differ systematically from the others, the conclusions may not carry over to them.
  \item \textbf{92.8\% join rate.} Claims whose beneficiary could not be matched on name and date of birth have no beneficiary information.
  \item \textbf{Sparse source columns.} \texttt{REINSURER} is genuinely blank in the source and was dropped.
  \item \textbf{One insurer, one country.} All results in this report come from Al Hilal Takaful (UAE) data; the Jordan sample is outside its scope, so nothing here shows that the findings transfer to another insurer.
\end{itemize}

