\chapter{Reflection and Conclusion}\label{ch:conclusion}

\begin{chapteroverview}
This chapter covers:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item a reflection on what worked, what was difficult and what would be done differently
  \item the conclusion and the answer to the essential question
\end{itemize}
\end{chapteroverview}


\section{Reflection}

\subsection{What worked}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Counting everything.} Recording every removed row and every excluded column made the pipeline auditable and turned vague cleaning into a clear funnel.
  \item \textbf{Scoping early.} Settling the MVP scope before building (single agent, static defender, synthetic-only sandbox) kept the work finishable in two months and left room for a useful bonus round.
  \item \textbf{A baseline for the attacker.} Evaluating a random policy first suggested that the defender, rather than the attacker, was the main problem. Without that comparison, the 90.3\% would have looked like a strong result.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What was difficult}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Silent failures.} A join on two ID columns that ran without error but matched nothing, and a metric (\texttt{kept\_fraction}) that was wrong only for caught episodes. Both were found by cross-checking, not by error messages.
  \item \textbf{Messy exports.} Title rows, corrupted defined names and reference sheets required dedicated loading functions.
  \item \textbf{Modest results.} The PPO agent barely improved on chance, and the retrained defender improved narrowly on real data. The honest choice was to report this and explain it.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{What I would do differently}
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item Build the random baseline and the peer-aware defender first, and run the attack against both.
  \item Fit every encoder on the training fold only from the start.
  \item Use several seeds and more episodes, and keep a reward-function description next to the code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Skills developed}
Data engineering on messy real-world files; handling extreme class imbalance and weak labels; leakage-aware, temporally valid evaluation; synthetic tabular data with CTGAN; reinforcement learning with Gymnasium and PPO; structured literature analysis; and scientific writing that states limits as clearly as results.

\section{Conclusion}
This internship followed the Challenge-Based Learning cycle. In \emph{Engage}, it framed the challenge of testing detection against fraud that has not happened yet. In \emph{Investigate}, for the literature review (jointly with Zayneb Troudi), it reviewed the key papers and their limitations, identified the research gaps and investigated the data. In \emph{Act}, it built a leakage-free pipeline from 1{,}017{,}993 raw claim rows to 839{,}209 usable claims with 614 recoveries, and a self-play red team in which a PPO fraudster, acting on 20{,}000 CTGAN synthetic claims, reached a 90.3\% attack success rate against a deliberately reduced XGBoost defender trained for self-play, while a random policy already reached 86.0\%.

The main finding is that the missing peer-group amount features of the defender, rather than the sophistication of the attacker, are the most likely primary weakness: 90\% of successful episodes were a single inflated claim, and a direct test against a peer-aware defender remains to be done. Folding the 337 discovered evasive sub-claims into training reduced the same attacker's success to 22.7\%, which closes one specific hole without proving general robustness.

Returning to the essential question, \emph{how can insurers make fair and trustworthy reimbursement decisions while distinguishing legitimate care from suspicious behaviour?}, the work contributes one concrete answer: test your detector against an adaptive attacker before someone else does, and read the results with their limits in mind. The result is a working minimum viable product with clear next steps (peer-aware defender, multi-round co-evolution, wider action space), not a complete co-evolution system.
